
====================================================================================================
sizetest.py  (ledger N40)  mode plan  --  writes the E1 plan folder (PF)
====================================================================================================
  script sha256   711dc0e6ac484121385a9d28090981ae1518f28f0cfe02e417086266abb6c4b3
  imports         refratio2 ad572d314971cff0  calprec2 3947d7ba86a55eec  costprobe2 ea5cb2e1d50e393a
  python          3.12.3
  numpy           2.5.3
  platform        Linux-7.0.0-38-generic-x86_64-with-glibc2.39
  workers         2 (this laptop process); --kaggle-workers 4 for the schedule
  rows            CONC (mechanism), TAU8 (boundary)
  ladder          50000, 200000, 1000000, 4000000   draws per (cell, N) 48
  near            |x - 1| <= 0.05 (5%)
  E1 seeds        calprec2.key_of(41, cell, N, rep); chunk ci: default_rng(SeedSequence(key + [ci]))
  bootstrap       B 10000, SeedSequence([20261030, 6, cell index, level index])
  controls        d 40 cells within 5%
  schedule        --shard-target 0.5 x --ceiling 21600 s; --stop-after 36000 s

====================================================================================================
What this does, in plain words
====================================================================================================
  CONC and TAU8 were calibrated so that a very large sample (64 million particles) has
  the target concentration. The runs use 50000-particle references, and in some cells
  those references miss the target. The suspicion is that the miss is simply what a
  50000-particle sample does at that beta. This script checks that in two ways.
  
  E0 only reads. calprec2 already solved beta on samples of 16000 and 64000 particles.
  If the suspicion is right, those betas sit on a predictable side of the run's beta.
  
  E1 measures, on Kaggle. It draws fresh starting samples of 50000 particles, and of
  larger sizes, from the same sampler, and computes their concentration at the run's
  beta. No algorithm is run: nothing moves, only the starting cloud is drawn and
  weighed. If the 50000-particle draws reproduce the run's miss and the miss disappears
  as the sample grows, the cause is the reference size. If fresh 50000-particle draws
  do not reproduce the run's miss, the cause is somewhere else.
  
  This plan draws nothing. It prints what E1 would cost, how it would be split into
  Kaggle jobs, and what a rerun with larger references would need measured first.

====================================================================================================
Gates and reads  (G3, G1, G2)
====================================================================================================
  G3 PASS  mechanism, boundary, exactcal match costprobe2.ARCHIVE_SHA256
  run  rerun-cal-s0of2-a1-kaggle-20261025-003238  3 files verified; CONC 1080, TAU8 1080
  run  rerun-cal-s1of2-a1-kaggle-20261025-050238  3 files verified; CONC 1080, TAU8 1080
  G1 PASS  CONC  144 cells, every mean equal to analysis_audit.csv
  G1 PASS  TAU8  144 cells, every mean equal to analysis_audit.csv
  G2 PASS  every CONC and TAU8 run beta equals calprec2_beta.csv's

